:answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29b22caa-a284-5c7f-8948-ff4f4a184ce4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96745" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain ${1,2}?$\n- **I.** $p:{1,2}\\to R$\n- **II.** $q:R\\to [1,2)$\n- **III.** $r:[1,2)\\to {1,2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is ${1,2},$ the set containing the two numbers $1$ and $2$ only.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[1,2),$ the interval from $1$ to $2$ (including $1$ but not including $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172032 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68007118-475d-53c7-b917-fb5e9c32659b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358662} {:answer/id #uuid "703ea606-a6cd-5982-8043-7ec8975c036e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358727} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ceb4b2-4e6e-57b2-9a17-7aa628732c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359320} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeb0f01-fa45-5137-a266-29127e8f23a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359680} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bdae4e-1d1d-53b8-b67a-f7b7dbe7c8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:[0,1]\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703ea606-a6cd-5982-8043-7ec8975c036e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358727} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28754de-f3b9-5f94-af58-77ca7f67bf70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31ddb8b3-b422-5344-82ab-b45add22b7cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96755" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set of real numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set of real numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:Q\\to R$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06831b3b-6061-5239-b68e-1d650bf34e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:\\operatorname{ln}⁡x↦x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357810} {:answer/id #uuid "42e066d9-1f10-53d9-a198-00433dbdd8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358323} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab1a5e0-00dc-5dcb-9e53-c8216361f504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358683} {:answer/id #uuid "77a366fc-cf13-5534-bc34-f99b1491e986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:\\operatorname{ln}⁡x↦x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358784} {:answer/id #uuid "954cafc2-e427-5b6f-b8b6-3c1bc034a37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab1a5e0-00dc-5dcb-9e53-c8216361f504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358683} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c6cc1d-2128-5296-a1bf-5ba24546bff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c4078cd-00a3-5cec-bb89-1bbd7fc0f112" :question/math-academy-id "q-96817" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x)=x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $h(x)=x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh:x↦x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1117a3-0b56-51e4-a666-3e78ae949ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident 