alue "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21f88362-c7c7-5137-8145-50557efa8aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469852} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd45a19-254c-54cd-b60d-66f146d091aa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af009dba-f7f5-5f73-94d4-82504fdd6317" :question/math-academy-id "q-96826" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:R\\to R?$\n- **I.** The domain of $g$ is $R$\n- **II.** The codomain of $g$ is $R$\n- **III.** The range of $g$ is $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:R\\to R\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $R$ to the set $R.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $R,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $R.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bda835-3f7e-5acf-b18b-2e089087e07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469860} {:answer/id #uuid "42cb668d-98a9-520e-b1e1-4fe002c1b694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42cb668d-98a9-520e-b1e1-4fe002c1b694" :answer/type {:db/id 